**New evidence**
- IMF July 2022 Article IV staff report
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Democratic Republic of the Congo: Staff Report for the 2022 Article IV Consultation, Second Review Under the Extended Credit Facility Arrangement, Request for Modifications of Performance Criteria, and Financing Assurances Review-Press Release; Staff Report, and Statement by the Executive Director for the Democratic Republic of the Congo","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-07-05","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/publications/cr/issues/2022/07/05/democratic-republic-of-the-congo-staff-report-for-the-2022-article-iv-consultation-second-520400","claim":"The DRC’s macroeconomic environment had improved since 2019, and the authorities had adopted prudent macroeconomic policies, most visibly by halting central bank financing to the government.","locator":"Summary, lines 268-270","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary IMF surveillance report with explicit country-year macro policy findings and direct authority attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly targets 2022 policy and macro conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Tshisekedi’s administration through its fiscal-monetary stance; the specific monetary implementation also involved the central bank.","contrary_evidence":["The same report says further strengthening of monetary and exchange-rate frameworks was still needed."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- IMF November 2022 mission statement on growth and inflation
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Staff Reach Staff-Level Agreement with the Democratic Republic of the Congo on the Third Review Under the Extended Credit Facility","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-11-18","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/11/18/pr22395-democratic-republic-congo-imf-staff-reach-staff-level-agreement-third-review-under-ecf","claim":"IMF staff said 2022 growth was forecast at 6.6 percent, largely supported by higher mining production, while inflation was expected to exceed 12 percent by end-2022 because of global food and fuel prices and supply-chain bottlenecks.","locator":"Press release summary and quoted statement, lines 224-229","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary IMF mission statement with explicit forecast numbers and causal framing for the year.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and about the same target period.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to the Tshisekedi government’s macro stewardship, but the report itself emphasizes external shocks and mining-driven growth.","contrary_evidence":["The growth figure was supported by mining output rather than broad-based household gains, and inflation was driven largely by external shocks."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- IMF November 2022 mission statement on reform agenda
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Staff Reach Staff-Level Agreement with the Democratic Republic of the Congo on the Third Review Under the Extended Credit Facility","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-11-18","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/11/18/pr22395-democratic-republic-congo-imf-staff-reach-staff-level-agreement-third-review-under-ecf","claim":"IMF staff said structural and governance reforms remained critical, including stronger anti-corruption and AML/CFT frameworks, tax simplification, mining transparency through publication of new and renegotiated contracts, and beneficial-ownership disclosure for government contracts.","locator":"Quoted statement, line 237","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary IMF statement on the government’s 2022 reform agenda with clear policy specificity.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Tshisekedi’s government as the reform agenda under review, though the quote is a policy recommendation rather than proof of full implementation.","contrary_evidence":["The source is prospective: it identifies needed reforms and does not prove they were fully carried out in 2022."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.7","5B.10"]}

- World Bank February 2022 Country Partnership Framework
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Bank Deepens Commitment to Building Human Capital and Inclusive Development in the Democratic Republic of Congo","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-02-22","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/02/22/world-bank-deepens-commitment-to-building-human-capital-and-inclusive-development-in-the-democratic-republic-of-congo","claim":"The World Bank endorsed a 2022-2026 CPF supporting Tshisekedi-era stabilization and critical governance reforms, including stronger public-financial management, oversight institutions to fight corruption, transparency and accountability, and a countrywide social safety net for poor, vulnerable, and conflict-affected people worth close to $1 billion and intended to benefit about 1.2 million people.","locator":"Body lines 146-155 and 149-150","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank country-program announcement with specific allocations and institutional objectives.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Covers 2022-2026 and explicitly frames the 2022 baseline and agenda.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Tshisekedi’s government priorities, though the World Bank is the implementing partner and the source is partly programmatic.","contrary_evidence":["This is a program framework, not a completed impact evaluation; it does not by itself prove realized welfare gains in 2022."],"lenses":["5B.5","5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}

- World Bank February 2022 on human capital, girls, and women
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Bank Deepens Commitment to Building Human Capital and Inclusive Development in the Democratic Republic of Congo","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-02-22","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/02/22/world-bank-deepens-commitment-to-building-human-capital-and-inclusive-development-in-the-democratic-republic-of-congo","claim":"The CPF rebalanced financing toward education and social protection, with investments in girls’ learning, women’s empowerment, and women’s economic inclusion, plus support for gender-based violence response and survivors’ services.","locator":"Body lines 152-153","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Same primary country-program source, but this is a distinct human-capital and distribution claim from the governance passage.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Targeted to the 2022 program cycle.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the government partnership under Tshisekedi, but the World Bank is the driver of the financing design.","contrary_evidence":["Again, this is a financing commitment rather than proof of 2022 implementation at scale."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8"]}

- World Bank June 2022 governance and connectivity package
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Bank Approves $750 Million to Support Critical Governance Reforms, Transport Infrastructure, and Digital Connectivity in the Democratic Republic of Congo","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-06-28","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/06/28/world-bank-approves-750-million-to-support-critical-governance-reforms-transport-infrastructure-and-digital-connectivity","claim":"The World Bank approved a $250 million budget-support operation for foundational economic governance reforms and a $500 million transport/connectivity operation, the first IDA-financed budget support in the DRC since 2005, to address public-finance, market-liberalization, forestry, transparency, and accountability issues.","locator":"Body lines 148-156","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank press release with concrete financing and reform details tied to the 2022 government.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Tshisekedi’s government because the financing was explicitly described as supporting its reforms; implementation still depended on ministries and WB projects.","contrary_evidence":["Budget support approval is an external endorsement of reforms, not independent proof that reforms were fully effective."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.4","5B.5","5B.6","5B.10"]}

- World Bank June 2022 roads and fiber-optic buildout
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Bank Approves $750 Million to Support Critical Governance Reforms, Transport Infrastructure, and Digital Connectivity in the Democratic Republic of Congo","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-06-28","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/06/28/world-bank-approves-750-million-to-support-critical-governance-reforms-transport-infrastructure-and-digital-connectivity","claim":"The transport/connectivity project would upgrade and pave 440 kilometers of climate-resilient roads in Kasai and North Kivu and lay fiber-optic infrastructure along the financed roads while supporting sector governance and local grievance handling.","locator":"Body lines 154-157","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Same World Bank release, but this is a separate infrastructure-implementation claim with precise targets.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and forward-looking from the 2022 decision point.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Tshisekedi because the project was part of his government’s development package and was described as supporting his administration’s integration agenda.","contrary_evidence":["The project was approved in 2022 but completion and distributional effects lay in the future."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.10"]}

- World Bank October 2022 economic update
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Democratic Republic of Congo Economic Update: Harnessing Growth Through Digital Connectivity to Create Jobs and Reduce Poverty","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-10-07","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/10/07/democratic-republic-of-congo-economic-update","claim":"Despite robust growth, poverty reduction remained elusive and growth benefits were not broadly shared; the report also said the DRC had a stark digital divide, low digital-economy indicators, and major geographic and socioeconomic gaps in mobile and internet access, while a 10 percent broadband increase could raise GDP by about 2.5 percent and generate close to 700,000 jobs.","locator":"Body lines 139-143","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank economic update with country-year analysis and concrete elasticity estimate.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to the Tshisekedi administration’s policy environment, but the source also emphasizes inherited vulnerability and external shocks.","contrary_evidence":["The growth and poverty pattern is heavily conditioned by mining, pandemic recovery, and external shocks, so ruler credit is partial."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- World Bank October 2022 telecom reform and private-sector agenda
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Democratic Republic of Congo Economic Update: Harnessing Growth Through Digital Connectivity to Create Jobs and Reduce Poverty","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-10-07","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/10/07/democratic-republic-of-congo-economic-update","claim":"The report said the government had liberalized the telecom sector through a new Telecommunications Law with a modern regulatory environment and universal-access goal, and it recommended secondary legislation, a national fiber backbone, PPPs, and fiscal/parafiscal reforms to simplify taxation and attract private investment.","locator":"Body lines 145-147","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank update with an explicit government policy finding and follow-on reform recommendations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Tshisekedi’s government because it describes government-led liberalization and the policy agenda around it.","contrary_evidence":["The report also says remaining challenges were substantial, so this is not a claim of solved structural problems."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.7","5B.10"]}

- World Bank December 2022 eastern stabilization project
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Democratic Republic of Congo: World Bank Approves $250 Million to Improve Stability and Resilience in Conflict-Affected Eastern Provinces","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12-22","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/12/22/democratic-republic-of-congo-world-bank-approves-250-million-to-improve-stability-and-resilience","claim":"A new $250 million IDA grant was set to give 3.3 million people in Ituri, North Kivu, and South Kivu improved access to community infrastructure and community-based reintegration programs while strengthening provincial administrations for the 17 million residents there.","locator":"Body lines 142-143","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank project announcement with quantified reach and implementation design.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022, though the benefits are prospective from the approval date.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Tshisekedi’s government strategy because the source says the project supports renewed government commitments and is implemented from the provincial level.","contrary_evidence":["The grant was approved in late 2022, so the source shows intended reach rather than realized outcomes."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}

- World Bank December 2022 stabilization context and anti-fragility design
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Democratic Republic of Congo: World Bank Approves $250 Million to Improve Stability and Resilience in Conflict-Affected Eastern Provinces","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12-22","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/12/22/democratic-republic-of-congo-world-bank-approves-250-million-to-improve-stability-and-resilience","claim":"The project targeted instability and fragility in eastern DRC, noted thousands of battle-related deaths in 2022, and emphasized community-based stabilization, reintegration, decentralizing governance, and improved transparency, accountability, and citizen engagement.","locator":"Body lines 144-150","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank project text with detailed operating model and context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about late-2022 conditions and the government’s response.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Tshisekedi because the project was described as aligned with the government’s renewed commitment and strategy for the region.","contrary_evidence":["The source underscores that conflict remained severe, which limits any claim of broad economic success in the east."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- Presidency September 2022 evaluation of the 145 territories program
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Réunion d'évaluation du programme de développement à la base des 145 territoires présidée ce jour par le chef de l'état.","publisher":"Présidence RDC","publication_date":"2022-09-15","url":"https://presidence.cd/actualite-detail/actualite/reunion_devaluation_du_programme_de_developpement_a_la_base_des_145_territoires_presidee_ce_jour_par_le_chef_de_letat","claim":"Tshisekedi chaired an evaluation meeting on the 145-territories development program; officials said the projects were ready and the plan would build hospitals, administrative buildings, and other infrastructure, nearly 200 works nationwide.","locator":"Paragraphs 71-74","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential office announcement of a named development program and concrete project list, though self-described.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Tshisekedi as the program’s chair and political sponsor.","contrary_evidence":["This is an official launch/evaluation announcement, not proof that the works were completed or well executed in 2022."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}

- Presidency December 2022 governors conference on territorial development
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"9eme conférence des gouverneurs: le président Tshisekedi se félicite de la stabilité des institutions provinciales et fait la promotion du \"pdl 145 T\".","publisher":"Présidence RDC","publication_date":"2022-12-22","url":"https://presidence.cd/actualite-detail/actualite/9eme_conference_des_gouverneurs_le_president_tshisekedi_se_felicite_de_la_stabilite_des_institutions_provinciales_et_fait_la_promotion_du_pdl_145_t","claim":"Tshisekedi said the 145-territories program was meant to promote territorial economies, improve rural living conditions through massive public investment, reduce rural-urban disparities, and was entering effective implementation with 2,200 projects.","locator":"Paragraphs 74-80","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official presidential statement with detailed program intent and implementation framing, but still self-reporting.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Tshisekedi because he articulated the program and claimed ownership of its implementation.","contrary_evidence":["The same article records complaints from governors about low own-revenue collection, weak transfers, and lack of provincial investment."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}

- Presidency October 2022 business-climate message
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"A Mbuji-Mayi, Le Président Félix Tshisekedi rassure les entrepreneurs privés de l'accompagnement de l'état congolais","publisher":"Présidence RDC","publication_date":"2022-10-28","url":"https://www.presidence.cd/actualite-detail/actualite/a_mbuji_mayi_le_president_felix_tshisekedi_rassure_les_entrepreneurs_prives_de_laccompagnement_de_letat_congolais","claim":"Tshisekedi told private entrepreneurs the state would keep improving the business climate to facilitate SME creation and innovative Congolese champions, while also visiting infrastructure works in the province.","locator":"Paragraphs 72-76","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential office report of the president’s business-facing policy message and accompanying field visit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Tshisekedi as a public commitment and development signal.","contrary_evidence":["The source is a political communication and not an independent assessment of the business climate."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.4","5B.6","5B.10"]}

- Reuters on missing funds at Gecamines
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DRC Dug $400 Million Deep","publisher":"VOA Africa / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-06-04","url":"https://www.voaafrica.com/a/drc-dug-400-million-deep/6603542.html","claim":"Reuters reported that more than $400 million in tax advances and loans that Gecamines said it paid to the national treasury could not be found, and that auditors also could not trace another $175 million bonus payment; the IGF report presumed the missing amount may have been misappropriated.","locator":"Opening paragraphs and audit-summary paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting on a government-audit finding, with direct numbers, named institutions, and corroborating detail.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirectly attributable to Tshisekedi because Gecamines is a state mining company under his presidency, but the article does not prove direct presidential involvement in the missing payments.","contrary_evidence":["Gecamines denied wrongdoing, and the report did not specify when the transfers were made."],"lenses":["5B.5","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- Primature on the September 2022 budget deposit for 2023
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Dans le respect du délai constitutionnel, le Premier Ministre Jean-Michel Sama Lukonde a déposé à l’Assemblée nationale le Projet de Budget pour l’exercice 2023 chiffré à 14,6 milliards de dollars américains","publisher":"Primature RDC","publication_date":"2022-09-15","url":"https://www.primature.gouv.cd/2022/09/15/dans-le-respect-du-delai-constitutionnel-le-premier-ministre-jean-michel-sama-lukonde-a-depose-a-lassemblee-nationale-le-projet-de-budget-pour-lexercice-2023-chiffre-a-146-milliards-de-dollars/","claim":"The prime minister said the government deposited the 2023 budget on time, valued at $14.6 billion, about 32 percent above the previous year, and said it would accelerate the 145-territories development program championed by President Tshisekedi.","locator":"Search-result snippet / opening paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government communication with a concrete budget figure and explicit link to the president’s development program, though the page was only accessible as a search result snippet in this session.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"A 2022 government action about the next budget cycle, within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Tshisekedi through his government and the program it explicitly tied to him.","contrary_evidence":["This is a budget proposal and announcement, not a completed spending outcome."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.6","5B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I reviewed roughly 30 documents across IMF, World Bank, the Congolese presidency and primature, Reuters/VOA mirrors, and related local reporting.
- I kept sources that directly described 2022 policy choices, program design, budget support, macro outcomes, social protection, infrastructure, business-climate messaging, and corruption/capture signals.
- I dropped generic country pages that only restated DRC poverty or development conditions without ruler-period specificity, plus duplicated World Bank/IMF pages that repeated the same facts without adding a new claim.
- I excluded later 2023-2024 material unless it was retrospective about a 2022 action; even then, I only kept it when it added direct evidence about the target period.
- I did not use structured WDI/WGI/V-Dem-style country-year series as citations because the local prior already supplied those facts and the instructions prohibited re-fetching them as evidence.

**Remaining gaps**
- There is still no strong direct evidence on whether Tshisekedi appointed and empowered especially competent economic professionals in 2022, beyond the general reform agenda.
- I do not yet have independent 2022 implementation data for the 145-territories program, the telecom reforms, or the business-climate pledges, only launch/evaluation and financing evidence.
- Household-level distribution data for 2022 remains thin: the best evidence points to persistent poverty and weak sharing of growth, but not to a fully measured welfare change by region or class.
- Corruption enforcement is visible in audits and reform rhetoric, but the record still lacks a clean chain from presidential choice to durable cleanup of capture.
- More search would help if you want a tighter view of `5B.2`, `5B.5`, or realized household impact, but it is unlikely to change the basic mixed assessment unless it turns up audit-backed implementation results or a stronger appointment record.
